<p class="text-lg text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
  There is no single national lemon law that sets one number of repair attempts for every new car. Each state writes its own statute. The repair count, the out-of-service time, who is covered, and whether a used car qualifies are in that statute, not in a slogan.
</p>

<h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Three laws that get mixed together</h2>

<div class="space-y-4 text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-10">
  <p>A state lemon law is a state statute. It usually runs through the motor-vehicle or consumer-protection title of that state's code. The attorney general or a state arbitration program is often the office that explains the process. The statute text, not this site, controls if the legislature has amended it. The state pages below were checked against those statutes on October 22, 2026, and the Texas page on October 23, 2026.</p>
  <p>The Magnuson-Moss Warranty Act, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/2301" rel="noopener">15 U.S.C. §§ 2301–2312</a>, is a federal warranty statute. It applies when a product is sold with a written warranty and the warrantor does not honor it. It does not enact a nationwide "three repairs or 30 days" test. The <a href="/guides/lemon-law/federal-lemon-law/">federal lemon-law guide</a> covers that act. The Federal Trade Commission explains the difference between a warranty and a service contract in <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/auto-warranties-and-auto-service-contracts" rel="noopener">Auto Warranties and Auto Service Contracts</a>.</p>
  <p>{{ recallRemedy.text }} A safety recall is not a lemon-law case. The recall statute is <a href="{{ recallRemedy.source }}" rel="noopener">{{ recallRemedy.citation }}</a>, in 49 U.S.C. Chapter 301. An open campaign can be a reason to visit a dealer. It does not, by itself, meet a state's lemon-law test.</p>
  <p>Used-car coverage, when a state has it, is often a different section with a shorter window than the new-car rule. Read that section before treating a used purchase as a new-car claim. The state page states the rule that was in the statute on the review date above.</p>
</div>

